1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,386
7,500 light-years away,
something is staring back at us.

2
00:00:05,936 --> 00:00:07,281
It has a head.

3
00:00:07,371 --> 00:00:09,035
Two pointed ears.

4
00:00:09,125 --> 00:00:11,174
And a single glowing eye.

5
00:00:11,824 --> 00:00:16,859
And the James Webb Space Telescope
has just shown us what it really is.

6
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,783
On October 29th, 2026,

7
00:00:22,823 --> 00:00:30,053
NASA and ESA released a new image from
two of the greatest telescopes ever built.

8
00:00:30,143 --> 00:00:34,047
Hubble and Webb, looking at
the very same patch of sky.

9
00:00:34,397 --> 00:00:37,939
Astronomers nicknamed it
the Cat's Head Nebula.

10
00:00:38,029 --> 00:00:41,102
And once you see the
cat, you can't unsee it.

11
00:00:41,452 --> 00:00:43,821
A dark, narrow pillar of dust.

12
00:00:43,911 --> 00:00:45,789
A head turned aside.

13
00:00:45,879 --> 00:00:47,863
A tail trailing down.

14
00:00:47,953 --> 00:00:49,234
And that eye.

15
00:00:49,584 --> 00:00:51,654
But this is no monster.

16
00:00:51,744 --> 00:00:55,591
It's something far stranger: a
snapshot of stars being born,

17
00:00:55,631 --> 00:00:58,955
and of the forces tearing
their birthplace apart.

18
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:02,607
First, where is it?

19
00:01:02,697 --> 00:01:08,076
The cat lives inside the Carina
Nebula, about 7,500 light-years away,

20
00:01:08,116 --> 00:01:11,957
in the southern constellation
Carina, the Keel.

21
00:01:12,107 --> 00:01:15,658
The nebula is roughly
300 light-years across,

22
00:01:15,698 --> 00:01:21,515
and home to at least a dozen stars
50 to 100 times the mass of our Sun.

23
00:01:21,665 --> 00:01:23,650
Here's the key idea.

24
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:27,495
Stars form inside cold
clouds of gas and dust.

25
00:01:27,585 --> 00:01:31,917
Gravity pulls clumps together,
until a new star switches on.

26
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:35,587
But the hottest, most massive
stars don't just shine.

27
00:01:35,677 --> 00:01:40,969
They blast out intense ultraviolet
radiation and powerful winds.

28
00:01:41,119 --> 00:01:45,898
That radiation heats nearby gas
until it literally evaporates.

29
00:01:45,988 --> 00:01:49,082
Astronomers call it photoevaporation.

30
00:01:49,232 --> 00:01:51,601
Thin gas vanishes quickly.

31
00:01:51,691 --> 00:01:54,273
Denser clumps resist longer.

32
00:01:54,363 --> 00:01:57,457
What's left are pillars,
sculpted by light.

33
00:01:57,607 --> 00:02:01,170
To see this scene properly,
we need two kinds of vision.

34
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:04,119
In visible light, dust is the problem.

35
00:02:04,209 --> 00:02:09,373
Tiny grains scatter and absorb it, so
thick dust looks like a black wall.

36
00:02:09,523 --> 00:02:11,849
Webb is built for infrared.

37
00:02:11,939 --> 00:02:15,187
Longer wavelengths slip
past many of those grains,

38
00:02:15,227 --> 00:02:17,678
so Webb can see where Hubble can't.

39
00:02:17,828 --> 00:02:21,007
Near-infrared looks
through the thinner dust.

40
00:02:21,097 --> 00:02:25,066
Mid-infrared sees the dust
itself, glowing with warmth.

41
00:02:25,216 --> 00:02:26,497
Three views.

42
00:02:26,587 --> 00:02:29,382
Three ways of seeing the same cat.

43
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:32,373
Start with Hubble.

44
00:02:32,463 --> 00:02:36,185
In visible light, the cat
is almost completely opaque:

45
00:02:36,225 --> 00:02:40,485
a dark brown pillar of dust,
blocking everything behind it.

46
00:02:40,835 --> 00:02:44,953
Along its right edge runs
a faint, hazy yellow rim.

47
00:02:45,043 --> 00:02:47,491
That's gas lit up and ionised by

48
00:02:47,531 --> 00:02:51,102
ultraviolet light from
nearby massive stars.

49
00:02:51,192 --> 00:02:55,011
It's consistent with
photoevaporation in action.

50
00:02:55,101 --> 00:02:57,854
The cat's skin is being stripped away.

51
00:02:58,204 --> 00:03:00,295
And the glowing red eye?

52
00:03:00,385 --> 00:03:05,207
Astronomers think it may be a young
star, still partly buried in dust.

53
00:03:05,557 --> 00:03:10,379
Hubble's filters pick out light
from oxygen, hydrogen and sulfur.

54
00:03:10,469 --> 00:03:15,254
That's why the background shifts from
blue and cyan at the top, to yellow,

55
00:03:15,294 --> 00:03:17,595
orange and red toward the bottom.

56
00:03:17,685 --> 00:03:20,481
It's a map of hot, excited gas.

57
00:03:20,831 --> 00:03:24,202
Now switch to Webb's near-infrared camera.

58
00:03:24,292 --> 00:03:26,639
Watch what happens to the dust.

59
00:03:27,089 --> 00:03:29,842
The pillar becomes more transparent.

60
00:03:29,932 --> 00:03:32,599
Its complex surface structure appears.

61
00:03:32,689 --> 00:03:37,191
And around it, a dense scattering
of stars, shown in blue and yellow.

62
00:03:37,541 --> 00:03:43,281
Even a few faint background galaxies show
up, seen partly through our own Milky Way.

63
00:03:43,631 --> 00:03:44,826
And the eye?

64
00:03:44,916 --> 00:03:46,111
Still there.

65
00:03:46,201 --> 00:03:49,295
Still conspicuous, even in infrared.

66
00:03:49,645 --> 00:03:53,358
Then comes mid-infrared,
from Webb's MIRI instrument.

67
00:03:53,448 --> 00:03:55,966
Now we're not looking through the dust.

68
00:03:56,056 --> 00:03:57,891
We're looking at the dust.

69
00:03:58,241 --> 00:04:01,377
Warm grains and
carbon-based molecules glow,

70
00:04:01,417 --> 00:04:06,114
outlining delicate globules and
strands on the cat's left side.

71
00:04:06,204 --> 00:04:11,431
Fragile structures, exposed to
radiation from nearby massive stars.

72
00:04:11,781 --> 00:04:14,299
The filament turns bluish gray.

73
00:04:14,389 --> 00:04:16,566
The background glows red.

74
00:04:16,656 --> 00:04:18,598
And the eye turns white.

75
00:04:18,688 --> 00:04:23,770
Those colours are assigned, by
the way: MIRI's filters at 7.7,

76
00:04:23,810 --> 00:04:28,587
11 and 15 micrometres are
mapped to blue, green and red.

77
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:33,482
So let's zoom in on that eye.

78
00:04:33,932 --> 00:04:36,983
If it is a young star, it's an infant.

79
00:04:37,073 --> 00:04:42,239
Stars like this form when a dense knot
of gas collapses under its own gravity,

80
00:04:42,279 --> 00:04:47,101
wrapped in a cocoon of dust that
hides the newborn from visible light.

81
00:04:47,451 --> 00:04:51,134
In Webb's mid-infrared image,
just to the right of the pillar,

82
00:04:51,174 --> 00:04:53,126
there's another bright source.

83
00:04:53,216 --> 00:04:55,542
Yellow, against a reddish glow.

84
00:04:55,632 --> 00:04:59,323
It's likely an infant star
too, embedded in dust.

85
00:04:59,673 --> 00:05:03,066
A narrow filament seems
to cross right over it.

86
00:05:03,156 --> 00:05:04,778
Are the two connected?

87
00:05:04,868 --> 00:05:07,727
Honestly, the image alone can't tell us.

88
00:05:07,817 --> 00:05:12,213
That's exactly the kind of question
astronomers will chase next.

89
00:05:12,563 --> 00:05:15,465
There's another puzzle
near the cat's belly.

90
00:05:15,555 --> 00:05:20,227
In Webb's near-infrared image,
there's a faint, jet-like feature.

91
00:05:20,317 --> 00:05:23,390
It could be gas blasted
out by a young star.

92
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:25,550
Or it could be something else.

93
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:28,051
Its origin is uncertain.

94
00:05:28,401 --> 00:05:32,711
And that uncertainty is what
makes this image so valuable.

95
00:05:32,801 --> 00:05:34,679
It isn't just beautiful.

96
00:05:34,769 --> 00:05:37,778
It's a catalogue of open questions.

97
00:05:38,228 --> 00:05:43,733
One quick warning: this is not the Cat's
Paw Nebula, which Webb imaged last year.

98
00:05:43,823 --> 00:05:48,389
That one sits in Scorpius,
roughly 4,000 light-years away.

99
00:05:48,479 --> 00:05:52,191
The Cat's Head is in
Carina, nearly twice as far.

100
00:05:52,541 --> 00:05:54,334
And how big is this scene?

101
00:05:54,424 --> 00:05:58,092
Hubble's view is about
2.4 arcminutes across,

102
00:05:58,132 --> 00:06:01,742
which works out to around 5.5 light-years.

103
00:06:01,832 --> 00:06:05,843
Light itself would need five
and a half years to cross it.

104
00:06:06,193 --> 00:06:11,442
And the light we're seeing left
this place about 7,500 years ago.

105
00:06:11,532 --> 00:06:13,389
Before the first cities.

106
00:06:13,479 --> 00:06:14,930
Before writing.

107
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:18,178
It has been travelling
toward us all that time.

108
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:34,421
Now for the part that makes
this cat truly haunting.

109
00:06:35,071 --> 00:06:36,650
It isn't permanent.

110
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:40,722
NASA and ESA are clear about this.

111
00:06:40,812 --> 00:06:43,565
The Cat's Head is a temporary scene.

112
00:06:43,655 --> 00:06:47,662
The same ultraviolet light and
stellar winds that sculpted

113
00:06:47,702 --> 00:06:50,503
its shape are still eating away at it.

114
00:06:50,853 --> 00:06:56,145
The denser material inside the pillar
resists erosion, at least for now.

115
00:06:56,695 --> 00:06:58,679
Think about what that means.

116
00:06:58,769 --> 00:07:02,845
The massive stars nearby are
both sculptor and destroyer.

117
00:07:02,935 --> 00:07:07,415
Their radiation helped carve the
cat, and it will help dismantle it.

118
00:07:07,765 --> 00:07:12,054
And yet, inside it, a young star
may be quietly switching on.

119
00:07:12,144 --> 00:07:17,137
The very cloud that's being destroyed
may be the nursery of something new.

120
00:07:17,487 --> 00:07:23,247
Creation and destruction, side by side,
in a shape we happen to read as a face.

121
00:07:23,697 --> 00:07:25,149
No creature.

122
00:07:25,239 --> 00:07:26,669
No monster.

123
00:07:26,759 --> 00:07:29,362
Just physics, light, and time.

124
00:07:29,452 --> 00:07:32,930
And for one brief
astronomical moment, a cat.

125
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:45,078
So the next time someone shows
you a face in the stars, remember:

126
00:07:45,118 --> 00:07:48,837
it's a star-forming cloud,
being pulled apart by light.

127
00:07:49,187 --> 00:07:53,279
Subscribe to Technical Talk India,
and tell me in the comments:

128
00:07:53,319 --> 00:07:55,502
what do you see in this nebula?

